From ce7a4718c2ea02b63713b08e4ca93ef423eab205 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 08:35:16 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] test: cover which leaf a control block names, and tr()
 merging

Three cases the earlier control block coverage could not see.

A PSBT that names each of two leaves with the other's control block is well
formed and parses, so a wallet is handed it and has to make sense of it. Signing
must hand on each block under the leaf the output proves it belongs to, because
recording both readings writes the same key twice and the result no longer
parses. decodepsbt is that parser, so the test reads the signed PSBT back
through it.

Combining a PSBT that names a leaf with another leaf's control block must reach
the same reading whichever side is merged into, and the transaction that comes
out must be the same one.

Finally, merging a tr() input must leave its control blocks alone. Truncating
one leaf's control block to its base gives a well formed depth zero block that
commits to a different output key and sorts ahead of the real one, which is all
it takes: a tr() signer picks the shortest block a leaf has without trying
another. Only one leaf gets it, since both leaves of a tree truncate to the same
bytes. The order that could finalize before still does.
